Sure! Here is an example of how to generate a table of contents (TOC) automatically in a LaTeX document:

First, you need to include the `toc` package in your document preamble, as follows:
```latex
\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{tocloft}
\usepackage{lipsum} % dummy text package
\begin{document}
```
Next, you can generate the TOC by using the `\tableofcontents` command, which should be placed after any `\maketitle` command:
```latex
\maketitle
\tableofcontents
```
Now, to add a section or a subsection, use `\section{<section name>}` or `\subsection{<subsection name>}`, respectively, followed by the text or content for each section/subsection. You can nest subsections within sections easily.

Finally, compile your LaTeX document using either PDFLaTeX or XeTeX, and your TOC will be generated automatically. The generated TOC will include all the sections and subsections in your document.
Here is the complete code example:
```latex
\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{tocloft}
\usepackage{lipsum} % dummy text package
\begin{document}
\maketitle
\tableofcontents
\section{Introduction}
\lipsum[1-5]

\section{Methods}
\subsection{Data Collection}
\lipsum[6-10]

\subsection{Data Analysis}
\lipsum[11-15]

\section{Results}
\subsection{Results with respect to data collection}
\lipsum[16-20]

\subsection{Results with respect to data analysis}
\lipsum[21-25]

\section{Discussion}
\lipsum[26-30]

\section{Conclusion}
\lipsum[31-35]

\section{Future Work}
\lipsum[36-40]
\end{document}
```
When you compile this LaTeX file using PDFLaTeX or XeTeX, the generated PDF output will include a table of contents page (Figure 1) with the section and subsection titles from your document.
