```java
public boolean isPowerOfThree(int n) {
    // Handle n<1 as it is not a power of 3
    if (n < 1) {
        return false;
    }

    // If n is a power of two, it is not a power of three
    if ((n & (n - 1)) == 0) {
        return false;
    }

    // The largest power of 3 can be stored in a 32-bit integer
    // which is 3^19 = 1162261467
    int maxPowerOf3 = 1162261467;

    // Any power of three can be iteratively reduced
    // to a base power of three using division
    while (n > 1) {
        if (n % 3 != 0) {
            return false;
        }
        n /= 3;
    }

    return n == 1;
}
```

This function first handles edge cases like n being less than 1, as such values are not powers of 3. Then, it checks if the input is a power of 2 using a bitwise operation, as powers of 2 would return 0 when ANDed with their value minus 1.

Next, it checks if the input is a power of 3 by iteratively reducing it to its base power if possible. The largest possible power of 3 that fits in a 32-bit integer is calculated and iterated over using a loop, checking if the input is divisible by 3 without a remainder each time. If the input can be reduced to 1 in this manner, the function returns true as it is a power of 3. Otherwise, it returns false.

This solution has a time complexity of O(log3(maxPowerOf3)) due to the iterative reduction phase.

